data: {"type":"response.reasoning_text.delta","output_index":25,"item_id":"rs_tmp_fikz9sxj44","content_index":0,"delta":"I now have an extensive body of scientific evidence. Let me compile this into a comprehensive output. Given my remaining context, I'll now produce the complete Lane B output with:\n\n1. Source packets (S-001 through S-NNN)\n2. Raw-bank lines for Banks 7 and 8\n\nLet me organize the claims I can extract:\n\nKEY CLAIMS FROM THE RESEARCH:\n\nMECHANISM - DOPAMINE/REWARD:\n1. Sugar/stimulants repeatedly release dopamine in nucleus accumbens (multiple sources)\n2. Intermittent sugar access causes dopamine release each time, unlike normal food where DA response habituates (Avena et al., 2008; Rada et al., 2005)\n3. Sugar bingeing rats show increased D1 and decreased D2 receptor binding in striatum (Colantuoni et al., 2001)\n4. D2 receptor downregulation mirrors effects of cocaine and morphine (Colantuoni et al., 2001)\n5. Sucrose infusion directly into NAc reduces D2 and mu-opioid receptors similar to morphine (Spangler et al., 2004)\n6. 94% of rats preferred sweet taste over cocaine (Lenoir et al., 2007)\n7. Intermittent sugar intake causes enkephalin mRNA decrease and mu-opioid receptor increase (Spangler et al., 2004; Colantuoni et al., 2001)\n8. In humans, low striatal D2 receptors correlate with prefrontal metabolism in obesity (Volkow et al., 2008)\n\nWITHDRAWAL:\n9. Naloxone-precipitated withdrawal in sugar-bingeing rats: teeth chattering, forepaw tremor, head shakes, anxiety (Colantuoni et al., 2002)\n10. Sugar withdrawal causes DA decrease and ACh increase in NAc - same pattern as opiate withdrawal (Colantuoni et al., 2002)\n11. Food deprivation for 36h after sugar bingeing produces anxiety and DA/ACh imbalance (Avena et al., 2008)\n\nCRAVING/CROSS-SENSITIZATION:\n12. After 2 weeks abstinence, sugar-bingeing rats lever press 23% more for sugar (Avena et al., 2005)\n13. Intermittent sugar access cross-sensitizes with amphetamine - hyperactive to low dose (Avena & Hoebel, 2003)\n14. Sugar-bingeing rats show increased alcohol intake (gateway effect) (Avena et al., 2004)\n15. Motivation for sugar incubates with abstinence (Grimm et al., 2005)\n\nFRUCTOSE MECHANISM:\n16. Fructose metabolized almost entirely by liver, unlike glucose (Lustig, 2013)\n17. Fructose drives de novo lipogenesis, hepatic insulin resistance (Lustig, 2013; 2020)\n18. Fructose does not suppress ghrelin - no satiety signal (Teff et al., 2004)\n19. Fructose increases functional connectivity of amygdala, hippocampus, orbitofrontal cortex (limbic/reward) vs glucose increases basal ganglia (Lustig, 2020)\n20. In obese youth, fructose effects on NAc dopamine severely attenuated - downregulation (Lustig, 2020)\n21. Fructose generates ROS 7x faster than glucose via Maillard reaction (Lustig, 2013)\n\nFAT+SUGAR COMBINATION:\n22. Fat+carbohydrate foods valued more than equally caloric fat-only or carb-only foods - supra-additive effect in dorsal striatum (DiFeliceantonio et al., 2018)\n23. Humans cannot accurately estimate calorie content of carb-containing foods (DiFeliceantonio et al., 2018)\n\nGUT-BRAIN AXIS:\n24. Sugar preference develops via gut-brain axis, independent of sweet taste (Tan et al., 2020)\n25. Vagal neurons respond to intestinal glucose but not artificial sweeteners (Tan et al., 2020)\n26. Silencing gut-brain sugar circuit abolishes sugar preference development (Tan et al., 2020)\n27. SGLT-1 is key intestinal glucose sensor for sugar preference (Tan et al., 2020)\n\nPREVALENCE:\n28. Food addiction prevalence ~20% overall (meta-analysis of 272 studies) (Praxedes et al., 2022/2024)\n29. FA prevalence: 14% non-clinical, 40% clinical samples; binge eating 63%, bulimia 84% (Praxedes et al., 2024 correction)\n30. Global free sugar intake: 12-14% energy in children/adolescents, ~11% in adults (Walton & Bell, 2023)\n31. WHO recommends <10% energy from free sugars, ideally <5%\n32. Average American: ~94g added sugar/day; fructose intake increased from 15g to 75g/day in last century (Lustig, 2020)\n\nCONTESTED CLAIMS:\n- Whether sugar is addictive in humans (Westwater et al., 2016 argue against)\n- D2 receptor downregulation in human obesity is CONTESTED (some studies find it, others don't)\n- Whether sugar addiction is a substance addiction vs eating addiction (NeuroFAST debate)\n\nI need to now produce the source packets and then the raw-bank lines. Given the massive amount of content, I'll focus on creating well-formed packets for the key sources and produce comprehensive bank lines.\n\nLet me create the output now.","sequence_number":1759}

